          "secNumber": "2.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.1 \u2014 Seafloor spreading and ocean crust structure",
          "content": "### Geological principle: what produces the pattern?\n\nPartial mantle melting at divergent boundaries supplies magma to layered basaltic crust, while tectonic extension and alteration modify idealized stratigraphy. An oceanic crustal column may include pillows, sheeted dikes, gabbro and altered ultramafic rocks, but tectonic exposures are incomplete.\n\n### Observation plan: what is actually measured?\n\nCombine ridge bathymetry, seismic velocity, heat flow and dredged or drilled lithology. Use magnetic anomalies and rock ages where available while recognizing hydrothermal alteration and tectonic rotations.\n\n### Alternative hypotheses and what would refute them\n\nA high seismic velocity is not definitive evidence of a particular rock without physical-property context. Oceanic core complexes expose deeper lithologies and depart from a simple uniform crust model.\n\n### Worked numerical investigation\n\n**Synthetic problem \u2014 Crust generation.** A ridge spreads symmetrically at 32 mm/yr per side for 1 million years; find one-sided width.\n\n**Equation and substitution:** `32*1e6/1000/1000`. The computed answer is **32 km**. The units in the input determine the units in the answer. Check the dimensional conversion before interpreting a scalar as a geological quantity. A physically meaningful result should also be examined at a second parameter value to determine whether the trend makes sense.\n\n**Interpretation:** One-sided width is half the full newly formed corridor if spreading stays symmetrical. **What this does not establish:** Actual plate motion and ridge geometry can change.\n\n### A focused field or laboratory investigation\n\nBuild and revise a crustal cross section using three independent data types and identify locations where drilling would test the proposed interpretation.\n\n**Design choice:** Inspect sensitivity to processing. Imagine regridding an acoustic field, changing a lithology classification threshold or revising one age control. Determine which apparent geological boundary persists and which vanishes. Report a stable core of interpretation as well as the disputed region, rather than selecting the single map with the neatest outlines.",
