          "heading": "Lesson 2.2 \u2014 Magnetic stripes and reversal chronology",
          "content": "### Geological principle: what produces the pattern?\n\nCooling volcanic crust locks in remanent magnetization as magnetic minerals pass blocking temperatures. Reversals of Earth's field may generate roughly symmetric magnetic lineations across spreading axes, allowing identification of polarity chrons and rates when the polarity timescale is constrained.\n\n### Observation plan: what is actually measured?\n\nUse profiles normal to a spreading ridge and compare pattern shapes after removing regional field components. Check ship heading effects, sample remanence and whether a polarity sequence has unique matches.\n\n### Alternative hypotheses and what would refute them\n\nMagnetization intensity and direction depend on mineral content, alteration and geometry; a magnetic low need not coincide directly with a single reversal boundary. Correlation by eye risks circular age assignment.\n\n### Worked numerical investigation\n\n**Synthetic problem \u2014 Half-spreading rate.** A dated magnetic boundary at 42 km from the ridge axis is 2.1 Ma old; find mean half-rate.\n\n**Equation and substitution:** `42/2.1`. The computed answer is **20 km/Ma**. The units in the input determine the units in the answer. Check the dimensional conversion before interpreting a scalar as a geological quantity. A physically meaningful result should also be examined at a second parameter value to determine whether the trend makes sense.\n\n**Interpretation:** One km per million years is one millimeter per year. **What this does not establish:** Age correlation, ridge jumps and plate-motion changes affect the estimate.\n\n### A focused field or laboratory investigation\n\nTest alternative chron correlations and compute a half-spreading rate from distance to a dated anomaly, stating both distance and time uncertainty.\n\n**Design choice:** Separate event from background. Estimate whether a measured layer or acoustic feature records a short-lived high-intensity event or the integration of many smaller processes. A process can dominate preserved sediment while occupying little elapsed time. Search for event boundaries, independent dates and reworked materials before extending the inference beyond one location.",
