          "secNumber": "2.7",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.4 \u2014 Subduction trenches and accretionary prisms",
          "content": "### Geological principle: what produces the pattern?\n\nAt convergent margins, a descending plate bends into a trench and may transfer material to an accretionary wedge or carry it deeper. Seismicity, fault geometry, sediment thickness and pore-fluid state all influence wedge deformation.\n\n### Observation plan: what is actually measured?\n\nInterpret bathymetric trench profiles together with seismic reflection images and earthquake depth distributions. Separate the plate interface from seaward normal faults and shallow sediment deformation.\n\n### Alternative hypotheses and what would refute them\n\nA steep slope may be created by erosion or recent mass wasting without tectonic uplift. Trench fill can obscure relief, and the wedge toe may be poorly imaged beneath disturbed sediments.\n\n### Worked numerical investigation\n\n**Synthetic problem \u2014 Trench sediment supply.** An incoming sediment layer is 0.8 km thick, 15 km wide, and moves 0.04 km/ka; find bulk volume flux.\n\n**Equation and substitution:** `0.8*15*0.04`. The computed answer is **0.48 km\u00b3/ka**. The units in the input determine the units in the answer. Check the dimensional conversion before interpreting a scalar as a geological quantity. A physically meaningful result should also be examined at a second parameter value to determine whether the trend makes sense.\n\n**Interpretation:** A cross-sectional area multiplied by velocity yields bulk transport volume per time. **What this does not establish:** Not all incoming sediment is accreted, and compaction alters bulk volume.\n\n### A focused field or laboratory investigation\n\nConstruct two end-member mass-balance scenarios for sediment accretion versus subduction and list data needed to constrain the real partition.\n\n**Design choice:** Replicate the observation. For this topic, describe which parts of a figure are directly measured and which were inferred from calibration, interpolation, geological priors or visual classification. The observation may be convincing even if the preferred explanation remains uncertain. Design a check in which a second observer can reproduce the result using the same input files and clear processing parameters.",
