          "secNumber": "2.8",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.5 \u2014 Seamounts, hotspots and ocean islands",
          "content": "### Geological principle: what produces the pattern?\n\nSeamount chains may record volcanism on moving lithosphere above mantle melting regions, but not all chains result from stationary hotspots. Erosion, subsidence and carbonate growth can modify volcanic shapes after eruption.\n\n### Observation plan: what is actually measured?\n\nCombine bathymetric geometry, rock radiometric ages, geochemistry, gravity anomalies and evidence of subaerial weathering. Check whether age progression remains monotonic within error.\n\n### Alternative hypotheses and what would refute them\n\nA linear volcanic chain alone does not uniquely recover plate speed. Changes in volcanic source position, multiple eruptive episodes and later tectonic motion complicate a single fixed-hotspot interpretation.\n\n### Worked numerical investigation\n\n**Synthetic problem \u2014 Seamount chain rate.** Two volcanic edifices are 310 km apart and differ in radiometric age by 5 Ma; estimate relative trend speed.\n\n**Equation and substitution:** `310/5`. The computed answer is **62 km/Ma**. The units in the input determine the units in the answer. Check the dimensional conversion before interpreting a scalar as a geological quantity. A physically meaningful result should also be examined at a second parameter value to determine whether the trend makes sense.\n\n**Interpretation:** Age-distance slope is a kinematic estimate in the chosen reference frame. **What this does not establish:** Hotspot movement and complex volcanism can affect the trend.\n\n### A focused field or laboratory investigation\n\nEstimate a hypothetical age-distance trend and compare the inferred rate with independent plate reconstructions before assigning a geodynamic mechanism.\n\n**Design choice:** Choose a competing model. List two geologically distinct processes that produce a similar measured pattern, then predict a variable on which those explanations disagree. Prefer the prediction with a measurable sign, geometry, age ordering or composition rather than a vague expectation of greater complexity. Specify both an inexpensive screening observation and a decisive but costly validation.\n\n### Research deep dive: resolving an apparent ridge migration\n\nA set of magnetic stripes seems symmetric across an oceanic spreading ridge. The first temptation is to label every local peak as one polarity reversal and compute plate motion. A better procedure starts with the geomagnetic polarity timescale independently of the profile and asks which candidate correlations match the ordering and spacing of reversals. A strip of positive magnetic anomaly can be widened by magnetized crustal thickness or altered by sensor geometry. One peak should never be treated as an exact reversal age with no error interval.\n\nSuppose two candidate dates yield half-rates of 18 and 25 mm/yr over a long interval. Both may fit noisy magnetics if one magnetic boundary is ambiguous. Test them with the predicted positions of additional anomaly reversals, fracture-zone traces or radiometric ages of recovered basalts. If independent evidence selects a rate, the answer is an average over the interval, not necessarily the rate of motion today. Kinematic rotations and discontinuous plate boundaries mean that a whole-ocean reconstruction is more than joining points on a single straight line.\n\nConsider a fracture zone that extends far beyond the active transform fault. Earthquakes may cluster only on the segment linking ridge axes, while the inactive continuation records a fossil age contrast between plates. Calling the whole long feature an active transform would overstate present hazard and misunderstand the relationship between ridge geometry and plate motion. Likewise the unusual lithologies of oceanic core complexes show that faults can expose deeper crustal or mantle rocks at some spreading centers; they are not necessarily exceptions that should be erased to preserve a textbook diagram.\n\nTo defend a tectonic interpretation, place observations on a plate-motion diagram with error bars. Describe which boundaries must be active, which may be relics, and which may arise from sediment cover or sonar processing. The hypothesis becomes stronger when it predicts an observation not used to construct it, such as independent magnetic polarity or an earthquake focal mechanism. This is the difference between matching a pretty geometry and testing a plate-tectonic mechanism.",
