          "secNumber": "3.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 3.3 \u2014 Turbidity currents and deep-sea fans",
          "content": "### Geological principle: what produces the pattern?\n\nDense sediment-laden flows travel downslope under gravity, generating erosion, bypass and graded deposition. Fan channels, levees and lobes reflect flow confinement, avulsion and changing sediment concentration through time.\n\n### Observation plan: what is actually measured?\n\nInterpret channel cross sections, bed grading, erosional bases and stacked lobes. Measure thickness and grain size along transects while accounting for later bioturbation or reworking.\n\n### Alternative hypotheses and what would refute them\n\nA fining-up bed is not uniquely a turbidity current deposit, and not every high-density flow behaves as an ideal dilute turbidity current. Trigger and transport mechanism are distinct inference problems.\n\n### Worked numerical investigation\n\n**Synthetic problem \u2014 Turbidite bulk volume.** A fictional deposit covers 22 km\u00b2 with mean thickness 0.4 m; obtain volume.\n\n**Equation and substitution:** `22e6*0.4/1e6`. The computed answer is **8.8 million m\u00b3**. The units in the input determine the units in the answer. Check the dimensional conversion before interpreting a scalar as a geological quantity. A physically meaningful result should also be examined at a second parameter value to determine whether the trend makes sense.\n\n**Interpretation:** Plan area times thickness gives bulk deposit volume. **What this does not establish:** Porosity and erosion mean bulk deposit volume differs from source volume.\n\n### A focused field or laboratory investigation\n\nContrast a channelized transport scenario with basin-wide hemipelagic settling and propose sampling locations that maximize discrimination.\n\n**Design choice:** Design a disconfirming observation. Suppose the leading geological hypothesis is wrong. Which new sample, repeat transect or independent instrument would most readily reveal the mistake? Prefer high-information contrasts over uniformly increasing sample counts; explicitly discuss how limited ship time changes the geographic representativeness of the result.",
