          "heading": "Lesson 3.5 \u2014 Passive and active continental margins",
          "content": "### Geological principle: what produces the pattern?\n\nPassive margins often preserve thick sedimentary prisms after rifting, while active margins show deformation and related hazards. Yet tectonic history, inherited faults and sediment supply mean the contrast is not binary.\n\n### Observation plan: what is actually measured?\n\nIntegrate basin subsidence curves, seismic fault geometries, sediment ages and crustal thickness constraints. Identify extensional, strike-slip and compressional episodes rather than labeling the margin from current seismicity alone.\n\n### Alternative hypotheses and what would refute them\n\nSalt tectonics and gravitational sliding can produce folds and faults without current plate-boundary compression. Conversely, an apparently quiet margin can host large submarine landslides.\n\n### Worked numerical investigation\n\n**Synthetic problem \u2014 Crustal stretching factor.** A crustal section thins from 32 km to 16 km; compute beta assuming local constant-area stretching.\n\n**Equation and substitution:** `32/16`. The computed answer is **2 dimensionless**. The units in the input determine the units in the answer. Check the dimensional conversion before interpreting a scalar as a geological quantity. A physically meaningful result should also be examined at a second parameter value to determine whether the trend makes sense.\n\n**Interpretation:** Beta is initial thickness divided by final thickness in this simplified approach. **What this does not establish:** Magma addition and depth-dependent stretching violate assumptions.\n\n### A focused field or laboratory investigation\n\nReconstruct a synthetic margin's sequence of rifting, subsidence and postrift burial, with at least one alternative chronology supported by available data.\n\n**Design choice:** Separate event from background. Estimate whether a measured layer or acoustic feature records a short-lived high-intensity event or the integration of many smaller processes. A process can dominate preserved sediment while occupying little elapsed time. Search for event boundaries, independent dates and reworked materials before extending the inference beyond one location.\n\n### Research deep dive: why deposition and transport are not synonyms\n\nA submarine canyon and basin-floor lobe make a compelling source-to-sink diagram. However, a canyon eroded in a low-stand setting may today have no active connection to a river mouth. An acoustic image showing a channel does not show the present flux through it. To test active transport, combine repeat survey geometry with contemporary moorings, sediment traps or dated deposits. A sediment core can show that some flows happened, but its preserved intervals do not independently reveal how frequently unpreserved erosional events occurred.\n\nA hypothetical river contributes ten million tonnes of sediment per year; the shelf stores four and an alongshore system exports three. The missing three million tonnes are not automatically delivered to the deep sea. They could appear as temporary suspension, unobserved burial, offshore transport, or a poor estimate of river loads. A mass-balance residual is an invitation to test the budget, not permission to invent the remaining pathway. Define the geographic limits and the timescale before interpreting fluxes.\n\nA turbidite layer may be thin in the proximal fan yet thicker in a distal depocenter due to erosion and bypass. Bed thickness alone therefore does not rank peak current strength. Graded bedding, erosional bases, traction structures, channel\u2013levee architecture and independent dating all contribute to the interpretation. A slump and a dilute turbidity current may occur within the same event sequence, so treating them as mutually exclusive categories can be misleading. A layer\u2019s trigger is a further question; an earthquake is only one possible cause.\n\nBottom-current drifts complicate the map. Persistent contour-parallel transport can sculpt elongate mounds and moats, while submarine gravity flows often emphasize down-slope pathways. A section across a drift may resemble a fan lobe; the along-slope continuity and sediment fabric help distinguish them. In a robust study, state when multiple transport processes may have operated on the same seabed at different times. A geological history that admits reworking can be more accurate than a neat, single-process cartoon.",
