          "secNumber": "5.2",
          "heading": "Geological framework: questions, scales and competing evidence",
          "content": "A constant accumulation-rate estimate is often convenient for classroom arithmetic, but the rate of burial changes across glacial cycles, currents, river supply and episodic slope failure. A well-constrained age-depth model should be monotonic where undisturbed sediment has accumulated, yet could contain hiatuses or age inversions from reworking. Magnetic patterns, fossil assemblages, stable isotopes and radiometric ages offer different kinds of stratigraphic information. Corrections applied to one proxy cannot automatically be applied to another. Cross-checks have to survive uncertainties in laboratory calibration, core depth registration, species ecology and chronology. Reconstructing past climate or tectonics from a core means asking which competing history could have produced the same measurements.",
