          "secNumber": "5.8",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.5 \u2014 Stable isotopes and paleoclimate proxies",
          "content": "### Geological principle: what produces the pattern?\n\nOxygen and carbon isotopes in marine carbonates encode mixtures of temperature, water composition, biological processes and carbon cycling. Proxy measurements are signals, not direct thermometers unless calibrations and contextual assumptions are met.\n\n### Observation plan: what is actually measured?\n\nRecord material, species, preservation, analytical standard, replication and calibration. Compare proxy records against carbonate preservation, microfossil assemblages and age-model uncertainty.\n\n### Alternative hypotheses and what would refute them\n\nAn oxygen-isotope change can reflect temperature, global ice volume or local seawater isotopic composition. Diagenesis or habitat shifts can move the observed signal without an equivalent climate shift.\n\n### Worked numerical investigation\n\n**Synthetic problem \u2014 Stable isotope contrast.** Two carbonate samples measure +2.3\u2030 and +1.1\u2030 relative to the same standard; find their difference.\n\n**Equation and substitution:** `2.3-1.1`. The computed answer is **1.2 per mil**. The units in the input determine the units in the answer. Check the dimensional conversion before interpreting a scalar as a geological quantity. A physically meaningful result should also be examined at a second parameter value to determine whether the trend makes sense.\n\n**Interpretation:** The difference is a measured ratio change, not a unique temperature difference. **What this does not establish:** Preservation, species and water-isotope composition affect interpretation.\n\n### A focused field or laboratory investigation\n\nDevelop a proxy interpretation table separating directly measured isotope ratios from inferred temperature, ice volume and circulation changes.\n\n**Design choice:** Replicate the observation. For this topic, describe which parts of a figure are directly measured and which were inferred from calibration, interpolation, geological priors or visual classification. The observation may be convincing even if the preferred explanation remains uncertain. Design a check in which a second observer can reproduce the result using the same input files and clear processing parameters.\n\n### Research deep dive: testing a paleoclimate story against time uncertainty\n\nSuppose a core at 2.0 m depth has an independently estimated age of 18 ka and a sample at 3.0 m has an age of 35 ka. A linear model gives one average sedimentation rate, but a hidden hiatus between those samples would invalidate interpolation for the missing time. Before fitting a smooth curve, inspect sedimentary contacts, core recovery, fossils and age-model residuals for discontinuities. A sample\u2019s depth should be recorded in the original core coordinate system; splicing separate holes into a composite depth record requires an explicit crosswalk.\n\nA large oxygen-isotope excursion can reflect a mixture of temperature, global ice volume, seawater isotope composition, species ecology and recrystallization. Reporting the measured per-mil difference is valid; converting it directly into a universal temperature change without calibration is not. Replicate analyses of well-preserved specimens, paired geochemical proxies and regional comparisons can narrow alternatives. Likewise a microfossil appearance may reflect ecological migration or preservation rather than the date at which the organism first evolved.\n\nMarine magnetostratigraphy can constrain the ordering of polarity reversals, but magnetic remanence may be overprinted after deposition. Demagnetization behaviour and rock-magnetic screening are essential before associating a pattern with a polarity chron. If more than one chron correlation fits, show the alternate models. The better reconstruction is the one that also agrees with independent dates and plausible accumulation-rate ranges, not merely the one producing the smoothest age-depth plot.\n\nA final stratigraphic synthesis should label three categories separately: what is directly measured, what age was assigned by a particular dating method, and what climatic or tectonic history was inferred. Show uncertainty in both the age axis and the proxy. A highly precise laboratory ratio with a broad age interval cannot identify the exact timing of a short geological event. The limitation is scientifically meaningful and should be visible in the figure caption.",
