          "heading": "Geological framework: questions, scales and competing evidence",
          "content": "An underwater slope can be steep, weakly consolidated or incised by faults without an imminent failure. Geological evidence can support screening for hazards but not credible site-specific probabilities unless calibrated with timing, material properties, pressure and forcing history. A landslide trigger is different from long-term preconditioning; multiple combinations of sedimentation, fluid flow and earthquake shaking may produce the same failed layer. Long-wave speed provides a first physical insight into tsunami propagation but is not an inundation forecast. Gas-hydrate stability depends on temperature, pressure, salinity and composition, not pressure alone. Studies supporting cables, energy facilities or public safety need professionally reviewed geotechnical data, not classroom charts.",
