          "secNumber": "7.7",
          "heading": "Lesson 7.4 \u2014 Manganese nodules, crusts and marine resources",
          "content": "### Geological principle: what produces the pattern?\n\nMetal-rich nodules and ferromanganese crusts accrete through combinations of hydrogenetic and diagenetic processes at very slow rates. Spatial coverage, grade and accessibility are distinct from economic or legally recoverable reserves.\n\n### Observation plan: what is actually measured?\n\nDocument sediment setting, substrate, size distribution, chemical assays, survey density and ecological co-occurrence. Note selection bias of photographed sampling locations.\n\n### Alternative hypotheses and what would refute them\n\nHigh surface abundance does not show continuous subsurface grade, ore recovery feasibility or acceptable environmental impact. A resource estimate needs representative sampling, regulatory context and economic assumptions.\n\n### Worked numerical investigation\n\n**Synthetic problem \u2014 Nodule growth time.** A conceptual layer grows at 3 mm/Ma; how long would 15 mm require at constant rate?\n\n**Equation and substitution:** `15/3`. The computed answer is **5 Ma**. The units in the input determine the units in the answer. Check the dimensional conversion before interpreting a scalar as a geological quantity. A physically meaningful result should also be examined at a second parameter value to determine whether the trend makes sense.\n\n**Interpretation:** Extremely slow net growth is consistent with a long geological process. **What this does not establish:** Growth is episodic and can include dissolution and detrital mixing.\n\n### A focused field or laboratory investigation\n\nCompare a photographed transect with a probability-based sample design and explain why neither justifies a commercial extraction recommendation without much more evidence.\n\n**Design choice:** Choose a competing model. List two geologically distinct processes that produce a similar measured pattern, then predict a variable on which those explanations disagree. Prefer the prediction with a measurable sign, geometry, age ordering or composition rather than a vague expectation of greater complexity. Specify both an inexpensive screening observation and a decisive but costly validation.",
